      en: "The Great Sphinx of Giza is one of the world's largest and oldest monumental statues, having guarded the pyramid complex for millennia. Carved from a single massive outcrop of natural limestone, it depicts a reclining mythical creature with the body of a lion and a human head, widely believed to represent the face of Pharaoh Khafre. The statue measures approximately 73 meters in length and 20 meters in height, facing directly east toward the rising sun. Throughout history, the Sphinx was frequently buried up to its neck in desert sands, which paradoxically helped preserve it from severe weathering. Between its front paws lies the 'Dream Stele' of Thutmose IV, documenting an ancient restoration effort. Despite erosion from wind and water, and the famous loss of its nose, the Sphinx remains a powerful symbol of mystery, strength, and wisdom. It is a central figure in Egyptian mythology and a highlight for any visitor to the Giza Plateau. Modern non-invasive scientific studies continue to search for hidden chambers and secrets beneath its weathered surface.",
